u.s president joe biden and russian  president vladimir putin are set to hold  talks on tuesday over rising tensions  between russia and ukraine the  announcement comes as large groups of  russian troops are reportedly gathering  at the border between the countries  raising concerns they could be preparing  to attack ukraine russia says it's  entitled to station its troops wherever  it wants and insists they pose no threat  focusing in  on what might lie ahead  these ukrainian soldiers are patiently  preparing for the worst here along what  many international experts say could  become the front lines of a full-scale  battle between russia and the ukraine  if russian troops attack us  we are ready to fight back  all of us are ready  in fact we've been waiting for this for  a long time  russia seized crimea from ukraine in  2014  sparking an ongoing war in the eastern  donbas region of ukraine bordering  russia  since the crimean peninsula was taken  moscow has backed separatists fighting  for control of the area  with the recent buildup of russian  forces on the border ukraine says it has  little choice but to prep for an attack  maybe they are just provoking us  maybe yes maybe no  nobody knows what our enemies are  thinking right now  because  the conflict has killed more than 13 000  people  including thousands of civilians  now residents feel they're being asked  to prepare for a full-out battle with  russia that's just too good what will  happen then  nothing good  it won't be the same kind of war as in  the past  they have nothing else to do but to  start a nuclear war with us  could ukraine lose the donbass region to  russia and if not how much further loss  of life could be necessary to ward off  the threat  international allies like the us and  others have voiced support but will that  be enough to protect ukraine or is  russia just flexing its muscles on the  borders again  looking to keep everyone guessing and  retaliation at bay  for more on this i'm joined by cardi  leek a senior policy fellow at the  european council on foreign relations in  berlin it's great to have you with us on  dw news how real is a threat of a  russian invasion of ukraine  i think it is real but not necessarily  imminent as i understand restroom  buildup  it is still first and foremost an  invitation to talk  but  the aim of race talks will be handing  moscow control over ukraine's future  political choices  which is very hard to do for anyone in  the west  not to mention immoral  and i think moscow means that they  seriously want to have a proper leverage  over ukraine  and they are prepared to achieve that by  military means if diplomacy given no  risk gives no results  well moscow has denied planning an  attack and also says it's entitled to  deploy troops wherever it wants so what  is putin's intention here  well i think putin's intention is the  same it has always been  he wants to exclude nato membership for  ukraine and have a firm leverage to make  sure that ukraine will never ever think  of anything like chris uh for several  years since 2015 moscow has hoped to  achieve  that goal by implementation of the minsk  agreements that imposed sort of  ceasefire and the active phase of war in  2015. however now they have probably  realized that the agreements are not  working as they hope  that ukraine even under presidency  lenski is not fulfilling the agreements  as moscow reads it  and at the same time they see that with  western military help ukraine is  becoming  a stronger and stronger  adversary even in military  means so  they simply want to put a stop on the  erosion of the leverage they had via the  minsk agreements and now they see it's  going away  now with these talks expected to come up  on tuesday how do you see president joe  biden uh taking these talks forward  it's a tricky one  because  by then  he has no intention to invite ukraine to  join nato but he's in no position to  declare the tall close to ukraine  forever after  so i do not think there is a good  solution to the situation that said  biden has very skillful diplomats i  think the us diplomacy right now  is is better than it has been for uh for  a long time  so  i guess that the creativity of freeze  people could come to some solutions but  i cannot properly think of  my advice would be to play for time try  to freeze the situation until moscow  comes to its senses and says that the  sort of control they want to have over  ukraine is impossible in principle not  because the west denies it but because  ukraine doesn't want it and  it is simply not doable in 21st century  the way it was possible after the cold  war after sorry after second world war  if things were to escalate and russia  was to invade ukraine  who would provide ukraine military  support who are its allies right now  well many nato countries have been  giving ukraine military support  not because they think of ukraine as a  future nato member but simply because  ukraine is a victim in that debate  i guess military aid could be supplied  in the future too but  it doesn't amount to boots on the ground  i think when it comes to actively  fighting the wall ukraine will be on its  own  okay cardi leak thank you very much for  your time and analysis
 

